{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2402 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "41a6852769ad48855392b540867aa3f3b7bd9760d3b77ddbdaf247b3fa0e5e6c" :edb/db-before-t 2401 :edb/db-after-t 2402 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535714 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:48:40.016Z" 13194139535714 true] [17592186147434 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362880 13194139535714 true] [17592186147435 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362887 13194139535714 true] [17592186147436 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362873 13194139535714 true] [17592186147436 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362894 13194139535714 true] [17592186148176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535714 true] [17592186148176 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y^{′} = (y + 4)(y^{2} - 4)$?" 13194139535714 false] [17592186148176 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+4)({y}^{2}-4)?$" 13194139535714 true] [17592186148176 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)({y}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y+2)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-2,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=\\pm 2.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-4,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-2,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/8756839e7b9c77934acec128d098eab5b7482a3b7422945b3507b6379fbc528b.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink\"." 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362874 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1089 "q-340357" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1100 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1113 #uuid "df140281-2a89-52e8-9118-441fdf457f4c" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362873 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/b2cb0682ba91ac23d418d91e7564cbb6245bf498de79b3e615f3ed2b1fcdb997.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362875 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362876 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362877 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362878 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362879 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1041 "selection" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362877 13194139535714 true] [17592186362874 1110 #uuid "b19d8b66-dfce-56e2-9a72-0d74061e4f9f" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362875 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/ef9ada6ab016d70b2b14d53cb2f3021793ea78dd69048700c07d5a5161901ecf.png" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535714 true] [17592186362875 1111 #uuid "07ee4cfc-dad1-5346-9bd2-a61b7d139e54" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362876 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c697a6b80923ffb6c557d59aa2e2924561121c373b4221709e280e5d35297e33.png" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535714 true] [17592186362876 1111 #uuid "7c760781-4e93-5dde-8d5e-d42dccf9d706" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362877 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535714 true] [17592186362877 1111 #uuid "9e652e9e-898d-5d56-abe8-96cc47e181cf" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362878 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c7e7fd7cbf8c3f2f9b68f0af3ecfbdb616730dc7b7cc3b03aa6f35228843080e.png" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535714 true] [17592186362878 1111 #uuid "8b94263f-3bb0-5fcb-a57f-181bf6a67121" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362879 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/514e6bec32ef0d172c5d3e9df05da8613806bb60faee267702ffa17238d0f826.png" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535714 true] [17592186362879 1111 #uuid "e8562a1e-14d3-5062-b9b5-794e11b25e42" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362881 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1089 "q-98714" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source." 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1113 #uuid "652ae185-a5d7-57a7-8788-52d6c37dcf05" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362880 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362882 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362883 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362884 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362885 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362886 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1041 "selection" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362885 13194139535714 true] [17592186362881 1110 #uuid "51e275b2-51af-57bd-8226-56df154e40cd" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362882 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535714 true] [17592186362882 1111 #uuid "0fbcf76d-9e9d-58c5-90a9-c1e96db05314" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362883 1034 "I only" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535714 true] [17592186362883 1111 #uuid "d8778424-d524-588d-9cdf-12d688db404b" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362884 1034 "III only" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535714 true] [17592186362884 1111 #uuid "d7814eb7-87a9-50fb-8f95-12bcdb11eb64" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362885 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535714 true] [17592186362885 1111 #uuid "d222cc4f-3028-5396-84d4-2afb969d0d20" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362886 1034 "II only" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535714 true] [17592186362886 1111 #uuid "abdbb513-2325-5722-a603-35c02cffa98c" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362888 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1089 "q-98761" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1100 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1113 #uuid "2466e8a8-bc2f-5b3d-8b9e-e87c3e64e5e5" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362887 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"." 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362889 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362890 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362891 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362892 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186362893 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1041 "selection" 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1104 #edb/ref 17592186362893 13194139535714 true] [17592186362888 1110 #u